_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 34
---
order: 34
---
short_label: X.34
---
statement: To find two straight lines incommensurable in square which make the sum of the squares on them medial but the rectangle contained by them rational.
---
red_highlight: yes
---
proof:

Set out two medial straight lines *AB* and *BC,* commensurable in square only, such that the rectangle which they contain is rational and the square on *AB* is greater than the square on *BC* by the square on a straight line incommensurable with *AB.* <div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX31/">X.31</a> ad fin.</div>

Describe the semicircle *ADB* on *AB.* Apply a parallelogram to *AB* equal to the square on *BE* and deficient by a square figure, namely the rectangle *AF* by *FB.* Then *AF* is incommensurable in length with *FB.* [!just VI.28; X.18]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="360" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.34 diagram" src="propX34.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.34",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,160",
            "B;point;free;220,160",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A,B,320,160",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C",
            "M;point;midpoint;A,B;0;0",
            "ADB;circle;radius;M,B;0;0;0;0",
            "E;point;midpoint;B,C",
            "F;point;lineSlider;A,B,190,160",
            "FD';line;perpendicular;F,A;0;0;0",
            "DD';line;chord;FD',ADB;0;0;0",
            "D;point;last;DD'",
            "semi;sector;arc;B,D,A;0;0;black;random",
            "tri;polygon;triangle;A,D,B;0;0;black;random",
            "FD;line;connect;F,D"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Draw *FD* from *F* at right angles to *AB,* and join *AD* and *DB.*

Since *AF* is incommensurable in length with *FB,* therefore the rectangle *BA* by *AF* is also incommensurable with the rectangle *AB* by *BF.* [!just X.11]

But the rectangle *BA* by *AF* equals the square on *AD,* and the rectangle *AB* by *BF* equals the square on *DB,* therefore the square on *AD* is also incommensurable with the square on *DB.*

And, since the square on *AB* is medial, therefore the sum of the squares on *AD* and *DB* is also medial. [!just III.31, I.47]

And, since *BC* is double *DF,* therefore the rectangle *AB* by *BC* is also double the rectangle *AB* by *FD.*

But the rectangle *AB* by *BC* is rational, therefore the rectangle *AB* by *FD* is also rational. [!just X.6]

But the rectangle *AB* by *FD* equals the rectangle *AD* by *DB,* so that the rectangle *AD* by *DB* is also rational. <div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX32/#lemma">X.32.Lemma</a></div>

Therefore two straight lines *AD* and *DB* incommensurable in square have been found which make the sum of the squares on them medial, but the rectangle contained by them rational.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

This proposition is used in proposition @X.40.
